{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/metrics.py:14:deprecated-method"], "search": "    return Metric.model_validate({"name": name.strip(), "value": value.strip()})", "replace": "    value_s = value.strip()
    try:
        value_v: Union[int, str] = int(value_s)
    except ValueError:
        value_v = value_s
    return Metric.model_validate({"name": name.strip(), "value": value_v})", "explanation": "Pydantic v2 smart-mode Union resolves an exact str input to the str branch, so '42' stays a string and numeric_total skips it. Pydantic v1 coerced left-to-right, turning '42' into int 42. The numeric conversion is done here, inside the function that the finding covers, so the Metric model's Union[int, str] annotation stays unchanged and out of the rejected scope. Keeps model_validate (the v2 replacement for parse_obj) from the earlier migration."}], "notes": "The failing test expects numeric strings such as '42' and '58' to become ints so numeric_total sums them to 100. Pydantic v2 smart-mode unions keep an exact str input as str, which is why the total is 0. The fix reproduces v1's left-to-right int coercion for numeric values only, and leaves non-numeric labels like 'n/a' as str. The edit is limited to the line the finding covers, because the previous attempt was rejected for touching lines outside the finding scope and imports. The earlier parse_obj -> model_validate migration is kept. The imports are unchanged: Union is already imported and field_validator is unused but was already present, so it is left alone. Nothing else was changed. Edge case: Python's int() accepts forms like '1_000' and '+5', which v1 also accepted, so behavior should match."}
